  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/c6de44233dec37e695e70902e0dace0771dd42a4cb87b45205345ba431e7774c.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $K?$",
  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/7c49639200c77c264ecb7b58e1eda4053b3ab0fe378d35caa3d3f8bdc6ad5027.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${K}^{\u2032}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${K}^{\u2032}$ is $r=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $K$ lies $2$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2).$",
